Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(\\ell_c\\) have slope \\(-1\\); consequently none of them is sunny.  \nThere are exactly \\(n\\) such lines, and for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have\n\\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_n\\) and contains **zero** sunny lines.  Hence \\(k=0\\) is admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Replacing a diagonal line by a sunny line  \n\nFix an integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\).  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(\\ell_c\\) are  \n\n\\[\nP_c=\\{(a,c-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le c-1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nChoose a rational number \\(\\alpha\\) that is **not** equal to \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) and that is also different from the slopes of any line already present in the configuration.  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\ns_c:\\; y-\\,(c-1)=\\alpha\\bigl(x-1\\bigr).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(s_c\\) passes through the point \\((1,c-1)\\in P_c\\) and, because \\(\\alpha\\neq -1\\), it is not parallel to \\(\\ell_c\\).  \nMoreover, for each integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le c-1\\) the point \\((a,c-a)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n\nConsequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_c=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\{\\ell_c\\}\\bigr)\n\\cup\\{\\,x=2,\\,x=3,\\dots ,x=c-1,\\,s_c\\,\\}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nstill consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and now contains **one** sunny line (namely \\(s_c\\)).  \nAll the newly added vertical lines have slope \\(\\infty\\) and therefore are not sunny; the only sunny line in \\(\\mathcal L_c\\) is \\(s_c\\).\n\nThus we have produced a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Induction \u2013 obtaining any number \\(k\\le n-2\\) of sunny lines  \n\nAssume that for some integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-3\\) we have built a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(t)}=\n\\{\\text{non\u2011sunny lines}\\}\\cup\\{\\text{sunny lines }s_{c_1},\\dots ,s_{c_t}\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ncontaining precisely \\(t\\) sunny lines and still covering \\(T_n\\).  \nSelect a diagonal line \\(\\ell_{c}\\) that is still present in \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\) and whose index \\(c\\) satisfies \\(3\\le c\\le n\\) and \\(c\\neq c_1,\\dots ,c_t\\).  \nApply the replacement described in \u00a72 to \\(\\ell_c\\).  \nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal L^{(t+1)}\\) has \\(t+1\\) sunny lines, still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, and still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe replacement can be performed as long as at least two diagonal lines remain untouched, because the points belonging to the extreme diagonals \\(\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) have no alternative vertical or horizontal lines that pass through **all** of their points.  \nHence the process stops after we have removed all diagonal lines except possibly \\(\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\).  \nSince the original family \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) contains \\(n\\) diagonal lines, we can perform the replacement at most \\(n-2\\) times.\n\nTherefore, for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Remarks on the remaining cases  \n\nThe above construction inevitably leaves the two extreme diagonals \\(\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) non\u2011sunny, because each of them contains a point that is not shared with any other diagonal line.  \nConsequently the method cannot produce configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).  \nWhether such configurations exist remains an open question.  \n\n---  \n\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) include all integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\).  The existence (or non\u2011existence) of configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) has not been settled."
