\) is admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Replacing a diagonal line by a sunny line  \n\nFix an integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\).  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(\\ell_c\\) are  \n\n\\[\nP_c=\\{(a,c-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le c-1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nChoose a rational number \\(\\alpha\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\alpha\\neq\\text{slope of any line already present}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\ns_c:\\; y-(c-1)=\\alpha\\bigl(x-1\\bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(s_c\\) passes through the point \\((1,c-1)\\in P_c\\) and, because \\(\\alpha\\neq-1\\), it is not parallel to \\(\\ell_c\\); moreover \\(\\alpha\\neq0,\\infty\\), so \\(s_c\\) is sunny.\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le c-1\\) the point \\((a,c-a)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nConsequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_c=\n\\bigl(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\{\\ell_c\\}\\bigr)\n\\cup\\bigl\\{\\,x=2,\\,x=3,\\dots ,x=c-1,\\,s_c\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers every point of \\(P_c\\) (by the vertical lines together with \\(s_c\\)), and still covers all the other points of \\(T_n\\) (they lie on the unchanged diagonals).  \nAll the newly added vertical lines have slope \\(\\infty\\) and are therefore non‑sunny; the only sunny line in \\(\\mathcal L_c\\) is \\(s_c\\).\n\nThus we have produced a configuration with exactly **one** sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Induction – obtaining any number \\(k\\le n-2\\) of sunny lines  \n\nAssume that for some integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-3\\) we have constructed a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L^{(t)}=\n\\bigl\\{\\text{non‑sunny lines}\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{s_{c_1},s_{c_2},\\dots ,s_{c_t}\\}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\ncontaining precisely \\(t\\) sunny lines and covering \\(T_n\\).  \nBecause at most \\(t\\) diagonals have been removed, at least two diagonal lines remain; in particular there exists a diagonal \\(\\ell_{c}\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\) that is still present in \\(\\mathcal L^{(t)}\\) and whose index \\(c\\) is different from all \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_t\\).\n\nApply the replacement described in §2 to this \\(\\ell_c\\).  \nThe resulting family \\(\\mathcal L^{(t+1)}\\) has\n\n* one more sunny line (the new \\(s_c\\)),\n* the same total number \\(n\\) of distinct lines,\n* the covering property (the points of the removed diagonal are now covered by the vertical lines together with \\(s_c\\)).\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L^{(t+1)}\\) satisfies the required conditions with exactly \\(t+1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe replacement can be performed as long as after the removal there remain the two extreme diagonals \\(\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\).  \nIndeed, the points of \\(\\ell_2\\) consist solely of \\((1,1)\\); this point is not covered by any vertical line \\(x=a\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) nor by any horizontal line, so \\(\\ell_2\\) must stay in the family.  \nSimilarly, the points of \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates ranging from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\); after we have removed all intermediate diagonals, each of those points is already covered by a distinct vertical line, but the line \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) itself is needed to keep the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).  \nConsequently we can perform the replacement at most \\(n-2\\) times, ending with a configuration that contains exactly the two non‑sunny diagonals \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_{n+1}\\) and \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore, for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Remarks on the unattained values \\(k=n-1,n\\)  \n\nThe construction above inevitably leaves the two extreme diagonals \\(\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) in the family; both have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore non‑sunny.  \nThus the method cannot produce more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe present argument does **not** prove that configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) are impossible; it only shows that the simple replacement technique cannot achieve them.  Determining whether such configurations exist remains an open problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe existence of configurations with \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\) is not settled by the present work."
