            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nA non‑sunny line can intersect \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points:\n\n* vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in \\((c,1),\\dots ,(c,n+1-c)\\) – at most \\(n\\) points;\n* horizontal line \\(y=c\\) is analogous;\n* line \\(x+y=c\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in \\((1,c-1),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\) – again at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nIf a configuration uses \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines, the total number of points that can be covered by them is at most \\(sn\\).  \nSince every point of \\(S_n\\) must be covered,  \n\n\\[\nsn\\ge |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad s\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(n\\); therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk=n-s\\le n-\\frac{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n-1}{2},\n\\]\nor equivalently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil } .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k_{\\min}\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x + (i-1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny) and passes through the lattice point \\((i,i)\\).  \nThe \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct because their intercepts \\(i-1\\) are distinct.\n\nEvery \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the points \\((i,i), (i+1,i+1),\\dots ,(i+n-1,i+n-1)\\) that stay inside the triangle).  \nThus the sunny lines together cover \\(k_{\\min}n\\) points.\n\n**Uncovered points.**  \nThe number of points still uncovered is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-k_{\\min}n\n= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k_{\\min}n\n= n\\Bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2}-k_{\\min}\\Bigr)\n\\le n,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nwhere the inequality follows from the definition of \\(k_{\\min}\\).\n\n**Non‑sunny lines.**  \nBecause each non‑sunny line can contain up to \\(n\\) points, we can cover the at most \\(n\\) uncovered points with exactly \\(n-k_{\\min}\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \nIndeed, for each uncovered point choose a line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) that passes through it and that has not been used before; there are infinitely many such lines, so we can keep them distinct.\n\nConsequently we have a family consisting of  \n\n* \\(k_{\\min}\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{k_{\\min}}\\);\n* \\(n-k_{\\min}\\) non‑sunny lines covering the remaining points;\n\naltogether \\(n\\) distinct lines that cover every point of \\(S_n\\).  \nHence the lower bound (5) is sharp: the minimal possible number of sunny lines equals \\(k_{\\min}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Obtaining all larger values of \\(k\\)\n\nStarting from the configuration just built (which has exactly \\(k_{\\min}\\) sunny lines), we can increase the number of sunny lines one by one:\n\n*Pick a non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\) that covers at least one point \\(P\\) which is not covered by any other line.*  \nSuch a point exists because otherwise \\(\\ell\\) would be redundant, contradicting the minimality of the construction.\n\nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by a new sunny line \\(\\ell'\\) that passes through \\(P\\) and whose slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and also different from the slopes of all already existing sunny lines (this is always possible because there are infinitely many admissible slopes).  \nThe new line \\(\\ell'\\) is distinct from all previous lines, the covering condition is preserved, and the number of sunny lines has increased by one.\n\nRepeating this operation we can obtain configurations with any number of sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\min},\\;k_{\\min}+1,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\nCombining the lower bound (5), the construction achieving it, and the replacement procedure, we conclude that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left\\{\\,\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\right\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
