rbidden.  \nHence **no sunny line can contain more than one of the three points** \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\).\n\nSince we have only \\(n\\) lines but must cover the three points, at least two of the lines must be non‑sunny.  \nConsequently, in any admissible configuration\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A convenient non‑sunny covering  \n\nFor every integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_c\\) have slope \\(-1\\) (hence are non‑sunny) and are distinct.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0=\\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\) and contains **no** sunny lines; it realises \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Replacing a line of slope \\(-1\\) by two sunny lines  \n\nFix an integer \\(c\\) with \\(3\\le c\\le n\\).  \nConsider the two lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ns_{c}^{(1)} &: \\; y=\\frac{c-2}{c-1}\\,x+2,\\\\[2pt]\ns_{c}^{(2)} &: \\; y=-\\frac{c-2}{c-1}\\,x+c .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBoth have slopes \\(\\displaystyle\\pm\\frac{c-2}{c-1}\\), which are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore they are sunny.\n\n*If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\)*, then solving the two equations in (2) gives exactly the two possibilities  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=\\bigl(a,\\;c-a\\bigr)\\quad\\text{with }a=1,2,\\dots ,c-1 .\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows that for each such \\(a\\) the point \\((a,c-a)\\) lies on **exactly one** of the two lines in (2):  \n\n* if \\(a\\le 2\\) it lies on \\(s_{c}^{(1)}\\);  \n* if \\(a\\ge 2\\) it lies on \\(s_{c}^{(2)}\\).\n\nConversely, if \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on either \\(s_{c}^{(1)}\\) or \\(s_{c}^{(2)}\\) then a short calculation forces \\(a+b=