            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines **iff** \\(k\\) is a non‑negative integer not exceeding \\(n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   - A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   - Let  \n\n     \\[\n     T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n     \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n     \\]\n\n   - For an integer \\(c\\) we denote  \n     \\[\n     V_c:\\;x=c,\\qquad H_c:\\;y=c,\\qquad D_c:\\;x+y=c .\n     \\]\n\n2. **Lower bound – at least two non‑sunny lines are necessary.**  \n   - Any sunny line meets each vertical line \\(x=c\\) in at most one point.  \n   - The columns \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) each contain at least one point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   - If only one non‑sunny line were present, that line could be either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n     * A vertical line misses the column \\(x=n\\).  \n     * A horizontal line misses the column \\(x=n\\) unless it is \\(y=1\\); in that case the point \\((n,1)\\) is covered but the point \