\mathcal F_{0}\\) by a sunny line that passes through its two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \n   The line through these points has equation  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\; y = -\\,x + 3 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(-1\\); however we need a **sunny** line, i.e. a line whose slope is *different* from \\(-1\\).  \n   To obtain a sunny line we keep the two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) but choose any line through them **different** from the diagonal; this is impossible because two distinct points determine a unique line.  \n   Hence we cannot replace a diagonal by a sunny line while preserving its interior points.\n\n   Instead we keep all diagonals \\(D_{c}\\) for \\(c=3,\\dots ,n+1\\) and add a sunny line that covers a point already covered by a non‑sunny line, thereby increasing the total number of lines to \\(n+1\\).  \n   To stay at exactly \\(n\\) lines we must delete one of the existing non‑sunny lines that is *redundant*.  \n\n   Observe that the point \\((1,1)\\) is covered simultaneously by \\(H_{1}\\) and \\(V_{1}\\).  \n   Consequently we may delete **one** of these two lines without losing coverage of any point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{1}= \\{\\,H_{1}\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,D_{c}\\mid 3\\le c\\le n+1\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,S\\,\\},\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(S\\) is any sunny line passing through the point \\((1,1)\\); for instance  \n\n   \\[\n   S:\\; y = \\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac12 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S\\) is sunny because its slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   The family \\(\\mathcal F_{1}\\) consists of  \n\n   * one horizontal line \\(H_{1}\\) (non‑sunny),  \n   * the \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) (non‑sunny),  \n   * the sunny line \\(S\\).\n\n   Hence \\(|\\mathcal F_{1}| = 1+(n-1)+1 = n\\) and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   The argument used for \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) shows that every point with \\(b=1\\) lies on \\(H_{1}\\); every point with \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) lies on the appropriate diagonal \\(D_{a+b}\\).  \n   The point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by the sunny line \\(S\\).  \n   Thus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\n   Therefore a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)**  \n\n   Lemma 1 already shows that any admissible configuration can contain at most one sunny line.  \n   Hence no configuration exists for \\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n\n   Combining the necessary bound \\(k\\le 1\\) with the explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\), we obtain the complete description  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\,}.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the problem is solved.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
