D\\) consists of points with a common value of \\(x+y\\).  \nAll together they form a partition of \\(T_n\\) into exactly  \n\n\\[\nm+m+1=n\n\\]\n\nnon‑empty subsets.\n\n*If \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).*  \nDefine\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_i &:=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}, && i=1,2,\\dots ,m+1,\\\\[2pt]\nV_j &:=\\{(a,j)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-j\\}, && j=1,2,\\dots ,m,\\\\[2pt]\nD   &:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=m+2\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow we have \\((m+1)+m+1=n\\) subsets, again a partition of \\(T_n\\) into \\(n\\) non‑empty parts, each of which is defined by a constant \\(x\\), a constant \\(y\\) or a constant \\(x+y\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 2.  From subsets to lines\n\nFor each subset \\(P\\) in the partition define a line \\(L(P)\\) by\n\n* if all points of \\(P\\) have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(c\\), let \\(L(P)\\) be the vertical line \\(x=c\\);\n* if all points of \\(P\\) have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(c\\), let \\(L(P)\\) be the horizontal line \\(y=c\\);\n* if all points of \\(P\\) satisfy \\(x+y=c\\), let \\(L(P)\\) be the line \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nBecause the subsets are disjoint, the resulting lines are distinct.  \nEach line \\(L(P)\\) contains every point of its defining subset \\(P\\); consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\(\\{L(P)\\mid P\\text{ in the partition}\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 3.  Verification of the required properties\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nExactly one line is produced for each of the \\(n\\) subsets, so we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nBy construction each \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to the unique subset that contains it, and therefore lies on the corresponding line. Hence the covering condition of the problem is satisfied.\n\n*Sunny‑line count.*  \nEvery line we have produced is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\nvertical lines are