athcal\n  P_n\\) (the line \\(y=1\\) contains \\(n\\) points, the others fewer).\n* A line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\); it contains exactly\n  \\(d-1\\le n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nHence each non\u2011sunny line covers at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered without using\nsunny lines is at most \\((n-k)n\\).  Since all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must\nbe covered, (1) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can contain more than two sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\\\\\n&H:\\;y=1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*If \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\), then \\(a\\le n-1\\) and the point\nlies on the vertical line \\(L_a\\).  \nIf \\(b=1\\), the point lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\).*\n\nHence the family (3) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains no sunny line.\nTherefore \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction with \\(k=1\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=\\frac12x+\\frac12 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) passes through the two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\); its slope \\(\\frac12\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor\n\\(-1\\), so \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.\n\nTake the following set of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=3,4,\\dots ,n-1),\\\\\n&L_1:\\;x=1,\\\\\n&\\ell .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of lines is \\((n-3)+1+1=n-1\\); we add the vertical line\n\\(x=2\\) to obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  The final family is\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1=\\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\;\\ell\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nCoverage:\n\n* For any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) we have \\(a\\le n-1\\) and\n  the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to\n  \\(\\mathcal L_1\\)).\n* The points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) lie on \\(\\ell\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered, and exactly one line\n(\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny.  Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction with \\(k=2\\)\n\nDefine two sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12 \\quad\\text{(through }(1,1),(2,1)),\\\\\n\\ell_2 &: y=\\tfrac12x+1 \\quad\\text{(through }(1,2),(2,2)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBoth have slope \\(\\frac12\\) and are therefore sunny.\n\nConsider the family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_2=\n\\{x=1,\\;x=3,\\;x=4,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\;\\ell_1,\\;\\ell_2\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe number of lines is \\(1+(n-3)+2=n\\) (the line \\(x=2\\) is omitted because\nits points are already covered by \\(\\ell_1\\) or \\(\\ell_2\\)).\n\nCoverage:\n\n* Points with \\(b\\ge3\\) have \\(a\\le n-1\\) and lie on the vertical line\n  \\(x=a\\) (which is present in \\(\\mathcal L_2\\)).\n* The points \\((1,1),(2,1)\\) lie on \\(\\ell_1\\).\n* The points \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) lie on \\(\\ell_2\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered, and exactly the two lines\n\\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) are sunny.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is attainable for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nFrom (2) we have the universal bound \\(k\\le2\\).  \nConstructions in Sections\u202f2\u20134 show that for each \\(n\\ge3\\) the values\n\\(k=0,1,2\\) can indeed be realized.  Therefore the set of admissible\nnumbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;2\\}} .\n\\]"
