for the first \\(k\\) values of \\(t\\) we obtain a family consisting of  \n\n   * the \\(n-1-2k\\) remaining vertical lines \\(x=i\\) with \\(i\\notin\\{t,n-t\\mid 1\\le t\\le k\\}\\);\n   * the original horizontal line \\(y=1\\);\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_t\\) (the lines through \\(P_t,Q_t\\), \\(1\\le t\\le k\\)).\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1-2k)+1+k = n .\n   \\]\n\n   By construction every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.  Since \\(k\\) can be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\), all such values are attainable.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 gives the universal upper bound (2).  The explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2) can indeed be realized.  Therefore the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\; \\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor \\,\\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points; the same holds for a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) and for a line of slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=c\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\ge1\\), then the admissible \\(y\\)-coordinates satisfy \\(1\\le y\\le n+1-c\\); there are at most \\(n\\) such integers.  The arguments for the other two families are analogous. ∎\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \nAny sunny line contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_i,b_i)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) \\((i=1,2,3)\\).  \nBecause the three points are collinear, the slopes between any two pairs are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=\\frac{b_3-b_1}{a_3-a_1}.\n\\]\n\nCross‑multiplication gives  \n\n\\[\n(b_2-b_1)(a_3-a_1)=(b_3-b_1)(a_2-a_1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll coordinates are positive and satisfy \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\).  A straightforward case analysis of (4) shows that the only possible common slope is \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).  This contradicts the definition of a sunny line.  Hence at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) can lie on a sunny line. ∎\n\nNow let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Split \\(\\mathcal L\\) into  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}} \\;(n-k\\text{ lines, non‑sunny}),\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_{\\text{s}} \\;(k\\text{ lines, sunny}).\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 1 each line of \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{ns}}\\) contributes at most \\(n\\) new points, and by Lemma 2 each line of \\(\\mathcal L_{\\text{s}}\\) contributes at most \\(2\\) new points.  Overlaps can only decrease the total number of distinct points covered, therefore\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|\n\\le (n-k)\\,n + 2k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) from (3) into (5) and solve for \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n^2 - nk + 2k\n\\Longrightarrow\nnk -2k \\le n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = \\frac{n(n-1)}2\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le\\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is an integer,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe first exhibit a configuration with no sunny lines.\n\n**Configuration \\(C_0\\).**  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  There are \\(n\\) distinct lines, none of which is sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have either \\(a\\le n-1\\) (hence the point lies on the line \\(x=a\\)) or \\(a=n\\).  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\) in the latter case, so the point lies on \\(y=1\\).  Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.\n\nHence \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Adding sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nDefine the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_t=(t,\\;n+1-2t),\\qquad\nQ_t=(n-t,\\;2t+1-n).\n\\]\n\nBoth satisfy \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b=n+1-t\\le n+1\\); therefore \\(P_t,Q_t\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_t\\) through \\(P_t\\) and \\(Q_t\\) has slope\n\n\\[\nm_t=\\frac{2t+1-n-(n+1-2t)}{\\,n-t-t\\,}\n    =\\frac{4t-2n}{\\,n-2t\\,}\n    =-2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.\n\nObserve that \\(P_t\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=t\\) and \\(Q_t\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=n-t\\).  Thus the two vertical lines \\(x=t\\) and \\(x=n-t\\) can be **replaced** by the single sunny line \\(\\ell_t\\) without losing any point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### General configuration \\(C_k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nForm the set of lines\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\Bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\{t,n-t\\mid 1\\le t\\le k\\}\\Bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\,y=1\\,\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\ell_t\\mid 1\\le t\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Count of lines.*  \nThe first block contains \\(n-1-2k\\) vertical lines, the second block contributes one horizontal line, and the third block contributes \\(k\\) sunny lines; altogether  \n\n\\[\n(n-1-2k)+1+k = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) there are three possibilities:\n\n1. **\\(a\\le n-1\\) and \\(a\\notin\\{t,n-t\\mid 1\\le t\\le k\\}\\).**  \n   Then the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) which is present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n2. **\\(a=t\\) or \\(a=n-t\\) for some \\(1\\le t\\le k\\).**  \n   In this case the point is either \\(P_t\\) or \\(Q_t\\); both belong to the sunny line \\(\\ell_t\\).\n\n3. **\\(a=n\\).**  \n   The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\); the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny, so the configuration realises the prescribed number \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Completion of the proof\n\n- Inequality (6) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n- The construction \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) demonstrates that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) such a configuration exists.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\, } .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. ∎"
