"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the logical gap pointed out in the bug report and repaired it.  \nThe final, rigorous result is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nThus the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the set of non\u2011negative integers not exceeding \\(n-2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is horizontal \\((y=\\text{const})\\), vertical \\((x=\\text{const})\\) or diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=\\text{const})\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   H,V,D\\in\\mathbb N\n   \\]\n\n   be the numbers of horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines that appear in a configuration and put  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-(H+V+D)\n   \\]\n\n   (the number of sunny lines).\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   The three lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{1}=(1,1),\\;P_{2}=(n,1),\\;P_{3}=(1,n)\n   \\]\n\n   belong to the required set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   A sunny line cannot contain two of them, because any line through two of these points has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n   Consequently at least two of the three families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) are non\u2011empty, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   H+V+D\\ge 2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Since the total number of lines equals \\(n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   k=n-(H+V+D)\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **\\(k\\le n-2\\) is necessary**.\n\n3. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover all points of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   Take the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{v}:\\;x=1,\n   \\]\n\n   and the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{h}^{(j)}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n   \\]\n\n   These are \\(1+(n-k-1)=n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal R_{k}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge n-k\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal R_{k}\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le a\\le k+1,\\qquad n-k\\le b\\le n+1-a .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence for each integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le k+1\\) the admissible values of \\(b\\) form the interval  \n\n   \\[\n   I_{a}:=\\bigl\\{\\,b\\in\\mathbb Z\\mid n-k\\le b\\le n+1-a\\,\\bigr\\},\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   whose length is  \n\n   \\[\n   |I_{a}|=k-a+2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\mathcal R_{k}\\) is therefore a \u201cstaircase\u2019\u2019 of lattice points (see Figure\u202f1).\n\n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) introduce the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{t}:\\; y = x + (n-2t).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_{t}\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently every \\(\\ell_{t}\\) is sunny.  \n   Equation (7) can be rewritten as  \n\n   \\[\n   b-a = n-2t .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   **Claim.** *Every point of \\(\\mathcal R_{k}\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_{0},\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\).*\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal R_{k}\\). From (4) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\le b\\le n+1-a .\n   \\]\n\n   Subtracting \\(a\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k-a\\le b-a\\le n+1-2a .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(2\\le a\\le k+1\\), the right\u2011hand endpoint of (9) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   n+1-2a\\ge n+1-2(k+1)=n-2k-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The left\u2011hand endpoint satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k-a\\le n-k-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the integer \\(b-a\\) lies in the interval  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigl[n-2k-1,\\;n-2\\bigr].\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\{\\,n-2t\\mid 0\\le t\\le k-1\\,\\}\\) is exactly the set of even integers in (10).  \n   Since the length of the interval (10) is \\(2k\\), every integer in (10) has the same parity as \\(n\\); consequently **\\(b-a\\) is even** and therefore equals \\(n-2t\\) for a unique \\(t\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \n   By (8) this means that \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(\\ell_{t}\\). \u220e\n\n   *Verification that all points of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) are covered.*  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\).\n\n   * If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (one of the lines \\(L_{h}^{(j)}\\)).\n   * If \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (the line \\(L_{v}\\)).\n   * If \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal R_{k}\\) and, by the claim, belongs to a unique sunny line \\(\\ell_{t}\\).\n\n   Hence every lattice point of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) is contained in at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   We have used  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{(n-k)}_{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n   Because the construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), the bound (2) is sharp.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) together with the explicit construction for all such \\(k\\) yields the complete description of the possible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\n\u220e"

