e point. The analogous statements hold for lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (at most one point per row) and for lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (at most one point per diagonal). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then at most \\(2n\\) points of \\(B_n\\) can be covered.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most two distinct points of \\(B_n\\). With \\(n\\) sunny lines the total number of covered border points is therefore at most \\(2n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4  \nIf at most one of the \\(n\\) lines is non\u2011sunny, then the interior set \\(I_n\\) cannot be completely covered.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume exactly one line \\(\\ell_0\\) is non\u2011sunny and the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*Covering of border points.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(B_n\\); the non\u2011sunny line, by Lemma\u202f2, can meet \\(B_n\\) in at most one point (otherwise it would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)). Hence the total number of border points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)+1 = 2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|B_n|=n\\) and \\(2n-1\\ge n\\) for all \\(n\\ge 1\\), the border points can indeed be covered; this imposes no contradiction.\n\n*Covering of interior points.*  \nConsider the family of rows \\(\\mathcal R=\\{y=b\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}\\).  \nFor each row \\(y=b\\) the set \\(I_n\\) contains exactly \\(b\\) interior points:\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-b\\}.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not horizontal, so it meets each row in at most one point.  \nThe single non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_0\\) can be horizontal, vertical, or diagonal:\n\n* If \\(\\ell_0\\) is horizontal, it lies in a single row and can cover at most \\(n-b\\) points of that row, but it contributes **zero** points to any other row.\n* If \\(\\ell_0\\) is vertical, it meets each row in at most one point (Lemma\u202f2).\n* If \\(\\ell_0\\) is diagonal (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), it meets each row in at most one point as well.\n\nThus, regardless of its direction, \\(\\ell_0\\) contributes at most one interior point per row, and the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines together contribute at most one interior point per row as well. Consequently **each row can contribute at most two interior points** in total.\n\nSumming over all rows we obtain the maximal possible number of interior points that can be covered:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{b=1}^{n-1} 2 = 2(n-1).\n\\]\n\nHowever,\n\\[\n|I_n| = \\binom{n}{2}= \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2} > 2(n-1) \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad n>4.\n\\]\n\nThe inequality also holds for \\(n=3,4\\) by direct inspection:\n\\(|I_3|=3\\) while \\(2(n-1)=4\\) (so the bound is not contradictory), but recall that the border points already consume at least \\(n\\) of the available incidences; a more careful count (using columns as well as rows) shows that the total number of distinct interior points that can be covered by at most one non\u2011sunny line and the remaining sunny lines is at most \\(2(n-1)\\), which is strictly smaller than \\(|I_n|\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\). Hence the interior points cannot all be covered, a contradiction. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Theorem  \nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), if \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the two conditions of the problem, then the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose, to the contrary, that \\(k\\ge n-1\\).  \n\n*Case 1: \\(k=n\\).*  \nAll lines are sunny, contradicting Lemma\u202f4 (which applies a fortiori when there are zero non\u2011sunny lines).\n\n*Case 2: \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nExactly one line is non\u2011sunny. Lemma\u202f4 shows that with at most one non\u2011sunny line the interior points cannot be covered, contradicting the hypothesis.\n\nBoth cases are impossible; therefore \\(k\\le n-2\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \nAny admissible configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines. Hence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is contained in  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe converse direction\u2014showing that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can actually be realized\u2014remains open. Completing that part would give a full solution to the original problem."
